// A type name is a name, not a prefix.
//
// `IsNumeric` asked whether a type name *starts with* "int" or "uint". Eight
// names is the whole set -- `int` and `uint` on their own are not types -- so
// the prefix looked safe. It is not: `"int32[]"` starts with `"int"`, and an
// array of integers was a number everywhere the analyzer asked.
//
// One root cause, nine wrong answers. Arrays could be added, subtracted,
// compared, masked, used as a condition, printed, formatted, assigned to a
// scalar and assigned from one -- none of which the backend can emit, so each
// either failed there with a message naming the backend's own mechanism and no
// line, or did something worse.
//
// The worst was `int32[][] rows = flat;`. Nothing refused it, and the backend
// read a dimension the descriptor does not have: **the compiler segfaulted**,
// with no diagnostic of any kind.
//
// The rank rule that came out of fixing it is worth stating on its own: an
// array may be seen at a *lower* rank than it has, because the storage is
// row-major and contiguous -- that is what lets the grouped literal
// `{{1, 2, 3}, {4, 5, 6}}` fill an `int32[]` of six, which
// tests/array-advanced.abs covers. It may never be seen at a higher one.

int32 sum(int32[] values) {
    int32 total = 0;
    int32 index = 0;
    while (index < values.length) {
        total = total + values[index];
        index = index + 1;
    }
    return total;
}

int32 main() {
    int32[] left = {1, 2};
    int32[] right = {3, 4};
    int32 scalar = 5;
    int32[][] grid = {{1, 2}, {3, 4}};

    // Arithmetic on arrays.
    int32[] added = left + right;
    int32[] scaled = left * right;

    // Ordering them.
    if (left < right) { return 1; }

    // Masking them.
    int32[] masked = left & right;

    // Using one as a condition.
    if (left) { return 2; }
    while (right) { return 3; }

    // Printing and formatting one.
    println(left);
    println(format("{}", right));

    // A scalar into an array, and an array into a scalar.
    int32[] widened = scalar;
    int32 narrowed = left;

    // And the one that crashed the compiler: claiming a dimension that is
    // not there.
    int32[][] rows = left;
    int32[][] alsoRows = new int32[2][];
    alsoRows = left;

    // The other direction stays legal, and is checked by
    // tests/array-advanced.abs; naming it here says the rule has a direction.
    int32[] flattened = grid;

    return sum(flattened) + added.length + scaled.length + masked.length +
        widened.length + narrowed + rows.length + alsoRows.length;
}
